
Perfect — since you want to implement RabbitMQ using Spring Cloud, that means you're likely looking for
a modern microservice-based approach where services communicate asynchronously via messaging, and RabbitMQ is used as the message broker.

This is typically done using:

✅ Spring Cloud Stream + RabbitMQ
Spring Cloud Stream abstracts messaging with binders (like RabbitMQ, Kafka), letting you define producers/consumers
as Java functions (`Supplier` / `Consumer` / `Function`) or via `StreamBridge`, without dealing directly
with low-level `RabbitTemplate` or ListenerContainers.

⚠️ Important (legacy vs modern):
- OLD / legacy: `@EnableBinding`, `@StreamListener`, `Source` / `Sink` → deprecated / removed
- NEW / modern: functional beans + `spring.cloud.function.definition` (or `StreamBridge` for on-demand send)


✅ What You’ll Build
A demo with:

- Spring Boot 3+ & Spring Cloud Stream
- One producer service
- One consumer service
- Messages passed through RabbitMQ using bindings


✅ Step-by-Step Spring Cloud Stream + RabbitMQ Implementation

🔸 1. Add Dependencies (Maven)
For both producer & consumer apps, add this to pom.xml:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-stream-rabbit</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Only needed on producer if you expose REST to trigger send -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

<dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-dependencies</artifactId>
            <version>2023.0.1</version> <!-- match your Spring Boot 3.2.x line -->
            <type>pom</type>
            <scope>import</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>
```


🔸 2. Define Message Payload (DTO)
Shared idea / same class shape in both apps:

```java
public record Notification(String to, String subject, String message) {}
```


🟢 PRODUCER APP

🔹 application.yml

```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: producer-service
  cloud:
    function:
      definition: send          # activates Supplier bean named "send"
    stream:
      defaultBinder: rabbit
      bindings:
        send-out-0:             # naming rule: <beanName>-out-0
          destination: notification-topic
          content-type: application/json
      rabbit:
        bindings:
          send-out-0:
            producer:
              routing-key-expression: '''notification'''

server:
  port: 8081
```

Binding name rule (very important):
- Bean `send` as `Supplier` → binding = `send-out-0`
- Bean `receive` as `Consumer` → binding = `receive-in-0`


🔹 Producer (Functional Style — auto-poll Supplier)

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class ProducerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ProducerApplication.class, args);
    }

    // Spring Cloud Stream polls this Supplier on a schedule and publishes each result.
    @Bean
    public Supplier<Notification> send() {
        return () -> new Notification("user@example.com", "Hello", "Message from Producer");
    }
}
```

Note:
`Supplier` is great for demos / scheduled emit.
For real APIs (send only when user calls REST), prefer `StreamBridge` below.


🔹 Better for microservices: on-demand send with StreamBridge + REST

```java
@RestController
public class MessageController {

    private final StreamBridge streamBridge;

    public MessageController(StreamBridge streamBridge) {
        this.streamBridge = streamBridge;
    }

    @PostMapping("/send")
    public String send(@RequestBody Notification notification) {
        // binding name must match application.yml (send-out-0)
        boolean sent = streamBridge.send("send-out-0", notification);
        return sent ? "sent" : "failed";
    }
}
```

If you use **only** StreamBridge (no Supplier bean), you can omit `spring.cloud.function.definition`
and still configure binding `send-out-0` in yml.


🟡 CONSUMER APP

🔹 application.yml

```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: consumer-service
  cloud:
    function:
      definition: receive       # activates Consumer bean named "receive"
    stream:
      bindings:
        receive-in-0:           # naming rule: <beanName>-in-0
          destination: notification-topic   # MUST match producer destination
          group: notification-consumers     # durable competing-consumer group
          content-type: application/json

server:
  port: 8082
```

Why `group` matters:
- With group → durable queue shared by instances (load sharing / competing consumers)
- Without group → anonymous queue (fine for quick demo, not for production)


🔹 Consumer Bean

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class ConsumerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ConsumerApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public Consumer<Notification> receive() {
        return message -> System.out.println("Received: " + message);
    }
}
```

Do NOT use `@EnableBinding` here — that is the old model.


